[157.558684, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m[Beginner]\u001b[0m Okay, let’s break this down! That “DEP-5 debian/copyright” file is basically a record of who created the software and how it’s allowed to be used, especially for Debian-based systems like Ubuntu.  It’s really important to keep this file because it helps ensure the software follows the rules and regulations for distributing it. Before you save it, carefully read everything to make sure all the correct information is there – specifically, who wrote the code and what license they used. A common mistake is forgetting to include the license details, which can cause problems later on!\r\n\r\n"]
[157.558752, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1mWrite debian/copyright to target directory? [y/N]:\u001b[0m "]
[164.282217, "o", "y"]
[165.981482, "o", "\r\n"]
