[318.332107, "o", "\r                                                                      \r"]
[318.332243, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m[MOTU]\u001b[0m Okay, let's break down what this Detect skill is doing – it’s a crucial step in ensuring your Ubuntu package builds correctly! Essentially, this tool utilizes the `Build-Depends` field, as defined in the Debian Policy Manual section 8.14 (“Package Dependencies”), to identify all the other Debian packages your application *requires* to compile and run. These dependencies – like compilers, libraries, and development tools – are listed in the `debian/control` file, allowing the Ubuntu packaging system (and later, users) to install everything needed for your software.  The tool scans your source code for dependencies like C/C++ headers and Python imports, accurately pinpointing these necessary packages, avoiding frustrating build errors later on.  You can find more details on this process within the Ubuntu Packaging Guide, specifically sections concerning dependency resolution and the `debian/control` file.\r\n\r\n\u001b[36m\r\nCalling Agent...\u001b[0m"]
[318.334339, "o", "\r\n"]
[318.33623, "o", "\r  ⠋ Detect: before save … (usually ~120s)   "]
